    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Adjectives after «sein» (predicative use)",
     "texto": [
      "German has a rule that makes grammar much simpler:",
      "1. **Predicative adjective (unchanging):**",
      "When the adjective comes after a verb like **sein** (to be) to describe the subject, it **never takes endings**, whatever the gender or number:",
      "**Der Mantel ist warm.** (The coat is warm).",
      "**Die Jacke ist warm.** (The jacket is warm).",
      "**Das Kleid ist warm.** (The dress is warm).",
      "**Die Schuhe sind bequem.** (The shoes are comfortable).",
      "2. **Contrast with the attributive position:**",
      "Only when the adjective goes *in front of* the noun does it take endings (*ein warmer Mantel*, *eine warme Jacke*).",
      "After **ist** or **sind**, the form always stays plain and basic: **schön, neu, alt, kurz, teuer, billig, bequem**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
